# Agent Dungeon Operating Context

## Purpose

Durable operating context for future agents working in Cloudflare Agent Dungeon.

This repo is a CascadiaJS demo plus long-running toy/game. It should remain real software, not throwaway stage goo.

## Operating loop

Joel is driving and learning. Agents should not bulldoze end-to-end.

Default loop:

1. Research one bounded mechanism from source.
2. Report findings and tradeoffs in plain language.
3. Implement the smallest useful slice.
4. Capture learning in `.brain/`.

## Canonical project memory

`.brain/` is canonical. Chat is not the memory system.

Use PARA:

- `projects/` — active outcomes, shaping docs, build logs, implementation plans.
- `areas/` — durable systems, vocabulary, operating rules, project doctrine.
- `resources/` — source-grounded research, API notes, handoff prompts, examples.
- `decisions/` — rare ADR-grade hard-to-reverse tradeoffs.
- `data/` — raw receipts / machine-readable artifacts.

Use `/skill:second-brain-execution` and `/skill:para-operator` when classifying or organizing durable context.

## Source mirror policy

Clone external source repos into project-local `.agent-sources/` as shallow mirrors/subtrees, then read local docs/source before claiming API behavior.

`.agent-sources/` must stay out of product commits via `.git/info/exclude`.

Existing important mirror:

- `.agent-sources/cloudflare-agents` — Cloudflare Agents SDK / Think source.

Effect work requires official Effect source at:

- `.agent-sources/effect`

Hydrate/search it before writing or reviewing Effect code.

## OSE / rules corpus policy

Old School Essentials and other RPG books are currently accessed through JoelClaw docs/PDF Brain.

Agent skills/playbooks are the runtime interface to the rules. They are not an RPG skill system. They are Pi-style instructions that teach a PlayerAgent or RefereeAgent how to play OSE: what procedure applies, what options are legal, what to ask, what to track, and what is private vs table-visible.

Start with:

```bash
curl -sS https://joelclaw.com/api/docs
curl -sS "https://joelclaw.com/api/docs/search?q=Old%20School%20Essentials&perPage=5&semantic=false"
curl -sS "https://joelclaw.com/api/docs/search?q=Old-School%20Essentials%20referee&perPage=5&semantic=false"
```

Rules:

- `RefereeAgent` may use `consultRules`.
- `PlayerAgent` should not get global rules/source access unless deliberately exposed.
- Public monitor output must not leak raw private/copyrighted corpus text.
- Internal events may keep doc/chunk IDs as receipts.

## Generative rails and volatility

The game is generative with minimal deterministic story rails. Skills/playbooks are the rails and guidewires.

Future agents should classify every rule/card/skill by volatility:

- core rules, privacy, and capability boundaries: change super rarely;
- deep lore/campaign truths: change rarely and through Referee Brain updates;
- OSE mechanics/procedures: change only with source receipts;
- situation skeleton: updated at setup or explicit maintenance beats;
- beat adjudication: updated through player intent, dice, and consequences;
- local color/improv: flexible table-facing texture.

Do not treat flexible color like law, and do not treat hidden truths or OSE procedure like improv.

## Project skills to create

Create project-local skills under `.pi/skills/`:

1. `ose-rules-access`
   - Query JoelClaw docs/PDF Brain.
   - Safe citation and non-leakage rules.
   - Referee-only access policy.

2. `source-mirror`
   - Clone/update source repos into `.agent-sources/`.
   - Exclude from commits.
   - Read docs/source before implementation claims.

3. `brain-maintenance`
   - Use `.brain/` as durable project graph.
   - Use `/skill:second-brain-execution` and `/skill:para-operator`.
   - Keep notes linked and source-grounded.

4. `think-agent-patterns` later
   - Capture idiomatic Think/Agents SDK patterns learned while building Referee/player agents.

## Project system prompt to create

Create `.pi/APPEND_SYSTEM.md` with:

- project identity and scope;
- Referee terminology;
- Think-first / Agents SDK idioms;
- fog-of-war as capability firewall;
- shared party workspace vs private player sandbox;
- research → report → implement → Brain capture loop;
- `.agent-sources/` source mirror policy;
- OSE access policy;
- Brain-first memory policy.
